<window type="dialog">
    <!-- Custom hex/RGB colour picker, launched via RunScript(color_picker.py, <kind>)
         from Settings > Colours (see lib/color_picker.py). Built from scratch rather
         than using Kodi's native colorbutton setting control, which only exists on
         Kodi 20 (Nexus) and newer; this addon supports Nexus and older.
         The 48-swatch grid (ids 300-347) is an 8-hue x 6-row HSV spectrum (grayscale
         ramp + 5 saturation/value bands); see lib/color_picker.py:_PALETTE for the
         exact hex value baked into each swatch's colordiffuse. A discrete grid rather
         than a continuous drag-to-sample gradient is deliberate: Kodi is commonly
         driven by a remote/d-pad with no pointer, and a button grid is fully
         navigable that way, while pixel-accurate gradient sampling would not be.
         No <font> is set on any control here: this addon ships no Font.xml for its
         minimal skin, so an unset <font> falls back to Kodi's guaranteed default GUI
         font rather than risking a reference to an undefined font id.

         Every focusable control has an explicit onup/ondown/onleft/onright: Kodi's
         automatic geometry-based navigation fallback (used when these are omitted)
         proved unreliable here across the mix of button/edit controls, so keyboard
         and remote/d-pad navigation didn't work at all until these were added.
         Within the 48-swatch grid, up/down/left/right all wrap at the grid's edges
         (e.g. top row up goes to the bottom row, same column); the rightmost column
         additionally bridges right/left to the field column at the matching row
         (row 0 <-> hex 205, row 1 <-> R 207, row 2 <-> G 209, row 3 <-> B 211, row 4
         <-> OK 230, row 5 <-> Cancel 231). The field column (205/207/209/211) wraps
         up/down among itself, with B (211) down into OK (230); OK/Cancel wrap up/down
         back to B (211) and the top of the field column (205), and left/right between
         each other. A control's onright/onleft pointing to its own id (e.g. 205's
         onright, 231's onright) is the standard Kodi trick for "don't move" at an
         edge, rather than leaving that direction unset and reintroducing the
         unreliable automatic fallback for just that one direction. -->
